<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brixen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bressanone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brixen, in Italian Bressanone, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where the Rienz joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a>. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Brixen from about 990, who ruled as prince-bishops until 1803, and its cathedral and bishops' palace dominate the old town. It passed to Italy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brixen.jpg" alt="Brixen" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver Abels · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brixen_Dom_Mari%C3%A4_Himmelfahrt_(BD_14187_3_05-2015).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
